WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.000
Did you see "Labyrinth" as a kid?&nbsp;
Have you rewatched it lately?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.280
You might pick up on some deep themes, subtle&nbsp;
references, and creepy overtones if you do.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.000
Almost every adult will recognize the voice&nbsp;
singing the song over the opening credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.600
of Labyrinth as that of pop culture icon David&nbsp;
Bowie, even before he appears in character as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.080
the Goblin King. Even today, Bowie remains&nbsp;
one of the most famous performers in rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.120
history — although unless they're music buffs,&nbsp;
younger kids are unlikely to know much about him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.080
While he may have been a highly influential&nbsp;
figure in music in the 20th century, kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.880
without an interest in rock and pop probably&nbsp;
aren't all that aware of his body of work.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.320
Of course, Labyrinth serves as a fantastic&nbsp;
introduction to Bowie as both actor and musician.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.520
His performance as the Goblin King continues to be&nbsp;
one of his most beloved, and it's no wonder, given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.200
that the character exemplifies the rock star glam&nbsp;
Bowie was so familiar with. Throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.320
Bowie played with persona and identity, and&nbsp;
although he was clearly playing a specific role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.000
in Labyrinth, he extended that tradition to the&nbsp;
film. The end result is an infinitely fascinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.240
and instantly iconic villain — one whose true&nbsp;
appeal only adults will be able to fully grasp.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.080
"Just fear me, love me, do as I&nbsp;
say, and I will be your slave."

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.240
Jennifer Connelly landed the lead role of Sarah&nbsp;
in Labyrinth after an exhaustive search that saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.360
all sorts of big names audition. In the end,&nbsp;
Connelly beat out a bevy of future stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.880
including Jane Krakowski, Yasmine Bleeth,&nbsp;
Sarah Jessica Parker, Mary Stewart Masterson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.960
Laura Dern, and Lili Taylor. At the time, Connelly&nbsp;
was only 14 years old and new to the entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.160
business, but Labyrinth wasn't her first film.&nbsp;
She had already starred in three other movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.320
although none of them cemented a legacy&nbsp;
as strong as Jim Henson's fantasy tale.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.360
Of course, adults will know that Connelly went&nbsp;
on to become a star and respected actor, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:01.120
who eventually won an Oscar for Best Supporting&nbsp;
Actress for her work in 2001's A Beautiful Mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.400
Today, Connelly tends to take roles in mature&nbsp;
projects that kids aren't too likely to watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.280
so they may not realize just what an impressive&nbsp;
career Sarah went on to enjoy. The funny thing is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.720
of course, that Connelly looks remarkably similar&nbsp;
today to the way she did in 1986, making her a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.840
highly recognizable star, even for kids that&nbsp;
only know her from this 35-year-old film.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.840
There are some huge names behind the scenes&nbsp;
of Labyrinth, from George Lucas to Jim Henson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.600
But one that many youngsters may not&nbsp;
recognize is that of Terry Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.080
who is credited with writing the film's script.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.800
Terry Jones was one of the members of the&nbsp;
groundbreaking comedy troupe Monty Python,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.560
and while the group's name may ring a bell&nbsp;
with some kids, they're unlikely to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:48.080
to put any faces to the name. Jones, who died in&nbsp;
2020, never had quite as high a public profile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.440
as other Monty Python members. However, he was&nbsp;
pivotal in shaping their unique brand of comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.360
and even directed or co-directed many&nbsp;
of their movies, including the iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.960
Monty Python and the Holy Grail. At the same&nbsp;
time, Jones became a noted children's book author,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.880
which is likely why Henson wanted him&nbsp;
to tackle the screenplay for Labyrinth.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.760
The irony, however, is that although Jones is&nbsp;
the only writer credited with penning the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.240
there were over 25 versions of the script written&nbsp;
between 1983 and 1985. Many of them weren't by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.040
Jones, either. In fact, it was a draft with&nbsp;
changes by famed screenwriter Elaine May that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.680
was used during production. In the end, due to&nbsp;
all the differences from his original screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.840
Jones believed that the finished product of&nbsp;
Labyrinth didn't feel very much like his story.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.320
Sarah is a pretty relatable character&nbsp;
for kids who know they've gotta grow up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.080
but who aren't sure they want to take&nbsp;
on the responsibilities of adulthood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.320
However, grown-ups are more likely to feel&nbsp;
that her petulance and lack of consideration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.160
for others are pretty insufferable&nbsp;
— as is this little catchphrase.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.440
"Oh, it's not fair!"

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.520
"It's not fair!"

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.560
"It's not fair!"

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.760
"You say that so often. I wonder&nbsp;
what your basis for comparison is."

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.600
Sure, she has some heroic qualities, including&nbsp;
intelligence, grit, and determination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.240
but her immaturity makes it hard to really&nbsp;
like her — especially early on in the film.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.440
It's possible that, if Sarah was younger, it would&nbsp;
be easier to forgive her youthful peevishness. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:19.600
at 16, Sarah seems a bit old to feel so put-upon.&nbsp;
After all, this is a character who gets mad at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.800
her baby brother for taking her teddy bear, and&nbsp;
whose room is full of toys and children's books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.080
It's not that adults would necessarily find Sarah&nbsp;
more likable if she acted like a grown-up, but she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.360
might be less frustrating if she actually took on&nbsp;
the responsibilities of being a teenager. But hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.960
in a sense, Sarah learning to do so is&nbsp;
kinda what the whole movie is about.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.720
The mythology behind Labyrinth is clear-cut:&nbsp;
when someone wishes the goblins will take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.280
someone else away, they happily grant their&nbsp;
wish, removing the unwanted individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.720
and making them a goblin too. But what's not as&nbsp;
clear is why Jareth, as the Goblin King, wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.080
to add to his already vast legion of goblins.&nbsp;
While he may be their king, Jareth seems to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.320
human — and perpetually annoyed by the goblins he&nbsp;
rules. So why would he want to turn Toby into one?

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.280
Despite this, Jareth does everything in his&nbsp;
power to make sure he can keep baby Toby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.280
first by offering Sarah everything she&nbsp;
wants in exchange for letting Toby go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.600
and then, when she rejects his offer, by&nbsp;
trying to thwart Sarah's rescue attempts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.960
While kids probably won't question Jareth's&nbsp;
motivations, many adults will recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.000
something doesn't quite add up here. Of course,&nbsp;
Jareth may be bound to uphold the tradition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.040
turning unwanted people into goblins to expand his&nbsp;
kingdom. But since the movie never mentions this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.440
it ultimately leaves you wondering just why&nbsp;
he needs another goblin in the first place.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.480
Kids could be forgiven for thinking that the&nbsp;
costumes and hair of the human characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:50.160
in Labyrinth are in keeping with the bizarre&nbsp;
fantasy land in which the story is set. Adults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:56.320
however, will recognize the film's style as being&nbsp;
emblematic of an even stranger time: the 1980s.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.920
The '80s had a distinct look and sound,&nbsp;
and the movie fully commits to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.720
Consequently, the costumes involve a lot&nbsp;
of vests, flowy shirts, puffy sleeves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.200
sparkles, and color. While Sarah's costumes&nbsp;
are relatively understated for the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.320
the Goblin King's are consistently over the top&nbsp;
— something that's especially obvious in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.200
hair and make-up. With elaborate eye shadow and&nbsp;
gravity-defying hair, Bowie's look in the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.040
is both iconic and a perfect encapsulation&nbsp;
of the stylistic excesses of the decade.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.160
The movie's '80s aesthetic&nbsp;
extends to its music, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.480
including the score by Trevor Jones and the&nbsp;
songs, which were written and performed by Bowie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.960
Featuring a whole lot of electric guitar and&nbsp;
synthesizer, the music may seem dated to younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:43.280
ears, but Bowie's songs are undeniably earworms.&nbsp;
"Magic Dance," in particular, is a silly yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.080
hugely memorable part of the movie — one that&nbsp;
will have even modern-day kids dancing along.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.240
From the movie's opening moments, Sarah's strong&nbsp;
attachment to fairy tales and fantasy is obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.120
And while kids may miss it, the play Sarah&nbsp;
is rehearsing at the beginning of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.360
is called The Labyrinth. This would indicate&nbsp;
that the plot of the movie is Sarah's fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.280
come to life, informed by the play she's spent&nbsp;
her time memorizing. Yet, the film slips so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.400
seamlessly from the real world of babysitting and&nbsp;
responsibilities to that of goblins and mazes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.880
that the land of Labyrinth comes across&nbsp;
more like a real place than a fantasy.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.040
Of course, that may be by design. For&nbsp;
someone who's as imaginative as Sarah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.440
the line between fantasy and reality might not&nbsp;
be all that strong. Nonetheless, that lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.520
clarity leaves a lot for adults to interpret&nbsp;
that kids are likely to overlook — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.400
how each of the characters exemplifies&nbsp;
Sarah's various fears and desires, and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.120
the story itself is a metaphor for learning&nbsp;
to accept your more adult responsibilities.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.320
At one point, as Sarah makes her way through the&nbsp;
labyrinth, she falls down a hole. The scene is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.560
reminisc–ent of Alice falling down the rabbit&nbsp;
hole at the beginning of Alice in Wonderland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.760
but in this case, the hole is lined with living,&nbsp;
stony hands that catch Sarah before she hits the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.720
bottom. The hundreds of hands lining the hole and&nbsp;
their penchant for coming together to form faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.440
and talk is undeniably compelling. In fact,&nbsp;
the sequence is generally a remarkable feat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:11.120
of creativity, one of the many moments that make&nbsp;
Labyrinth so captivating for both kids and adults.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.000
At the same time, though, it's&nbsp;
hard not to look at all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.920
hands gripping the teenage Sarah and&nbsp;
think about the matter of consent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.480
Given that those hands appear to be conscious —&nbsp;
and not particularly worried about where they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.560
grabbing her — adults may find the sequence&nbsp;
a little more disturbing than the kids.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.720
There's a lot to love about Labyrinth, but the&nbsp;
relationship between the Goblin King and Sarah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:38.240
shades into unsettling at times. Essayist&nbsp;
Alison Stine noted in The Atlantic that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.960
from the movie's outset, Jareth clearly&nbsp;
doesn't just want Toby. As she wrote:

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.560
"He wants Sarah. He wants her to love him, and his&nbsp;
longing increases, becoming more and more creepily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:54.480
clear, as the movie progresses. There 's both a&nbsp;
paternal appeal and stranger-danger in Jareth."

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.760
As an adult, this is more&nbsp;
than a little disconcerting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.880
and no sequence in the movie is more troublesome&nbsp;
than the scene in which Sarah imagines attending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.000
a masquerade ball. Over a song about falling&nbsp;
in love, the masked Goblin King makes a beeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.680
for Sarah. And as they dance, the charge between&nbsp;
them doesn't feel at all appropriate. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.680
for kids and even teenagers, the Goblin&nbsp;
King's menacing magnetism may make him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.960
compelling — even attractive. Adults, though,&nbsp;
will be more likely to recognize the disturbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.520
undertones this evokes and see Jareth for&nbsp;
the genuinely creepy villain that he is.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.560
When Sarah finally makes it to the Goblin King's&nbsp;
castle, she finds herself trying to get to both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:38.480
Toby and Jareth in a room right out of Relativity,&nbsp;
one of M.C. Escher's most famous works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.440
The black and white lithograph depicts a room full&nbsp;
of staircases, but only some seem to obey gravity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.240
while others go up or to the side. Meanwhile, the&nbsp;
people walking along the staircases and standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.680
on the landings all seem to conform to different&nbsp;
gravitational pulls. The idea Escher so vividly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.320
brought to life in his art is envisioned&nbsp;
in live action in the scene in Labyrinth.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.000
This is something adults and possibly&nbsp;
even some kids will notice instantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.240
although younger children who aren't familiar&nbsp;
with the art world won't understand the reference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.320
Still, there are clues in the movie&nbsp;
that this is a deliberate homage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.000
Not only does a print of Relativity&nbsp;
hang on the wall of Sarah's room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:19.360
the closing credits of the film include a&nbsp;
special entry thanking the estate of M.C. Escher.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.360
Sarah may not be the easiest character&nbsp;
to like, but she learns some valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.160
lessons during her adventures in the labyrinth.&nbsp;
These are lessons that kids may appreciate too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.800
even if they won't be nearly as aware of them as&nbsp;
adults will. Much of what Sarah learns centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:37.840
on the realities of life when an adult isn't&nbsp;
there to save you. For example, Sarah frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.720
complains that what she experiences isn't fair.&nbsp;
But when Hoggle makes a similar complaint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.920
Sarah realizes that he's right — sometimes&nbsp;
life isn't fair and that's just the way it is.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.280
"It's not fair!"

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.960
"No, it isn't. But that's the way it is."

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:55.840
After that, she stops complaining about fairness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.120
and faces the challenges of the&nbsp;
labyrinth head-on, fair or not.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:02.720
By the end of the film, Sarah is able to&nbsp;
recognize that, even though her journey has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:06.800
left her more mature and capable, she still&nbsp;
needs the friends she made along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:09.840
who are able to bring enjoyment&nbsp;
and whimsy into her life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.320
It's a smart but subtle lesson about the need to&nbsp;
balance the responsibilities of adulthood with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.440
freewheeling fun every now and then… something&nbsp;
even the most serious adult needs to remember.

